            <chunk name="Object" index="1">
              <items count="3">
                <item name="GUID" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">53dc2784-2bf9-4588-910d-fc3d198dfb06</item>
                <item name="Lib" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">82b21a65-da8f-433c-93fb-f9814609e1d6</item>
                <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Download Weather</item>
              </items>
              <chunks count="1">
                <chunk name="Container">
                  <items count="6">
                    <item name="ChartMonthFrom" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">1</item>
                    <item name="ChartMonthTo" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">12</item>
                    <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Pick an EPW weather file from a bundled catalog of 60,867 climate files — every TMYx station on climate.onebuilding.org plus the EnergyPlus store (IWEC, TMY3, CWEC, SWERA, …) — with your own folders and Morph Weather results listed alongside them; its second list, Built for this site, holds the sources that build a file for your site (Open-Meteo, PVGIS, NASA POWER, Copernicus CDS, NSRDB). Click the Station widget and choose Weather file (catalog)… for a searchable table with a map, or type a name, WMO id, direct URL or a full address. The catalog is searched offline; only the chosen file is downloaded, once, into the weather cache. An address no file is named after is the one text that leaves the machine: it is sent to OpenStreetMap's search (Nominatim, then Photon) to be located, and the nearest file is taken. The catalog browser's unit setting (SI or Imperial) changes only what is displayed — the Message strip, the match list and the Info text; the EPW file and every number this component outputs stay SI, so wire them to other components as before.

Version 1.22.0.827

Version 1.22.0.827

Version 1.22.0.827

Version 1.22.0.827

Version 1.22.0.827

Version 1.22.0.827

Version 1.22.0.827</item>
                    <item name="InstanceGuid" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">40b7597f-50f7-4886-8923-e6c99d4472d7</item>
                    <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Download Weather</item>
                    <item name="NickName" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Download Weather</item>
                  </items>
                  <chunks count="12">
                    <chunk name="Attributes">
                      <items count="2">
                        <item name="Bounds" type_name="gh_drawing_rectanglef" type_code="35">
                          <X>737</X>
                          <Y>340</Y>
                          <W>365</W>
                          <H>166</H>
                        </item>
                        <item name="Pivot" type_name="gh_drawing_pointf" type_code="31">
                          <X>791</X>
                          <Y>412</Y>
                        </item>
                      </items>
                    </chunk>
                    <chunk name="param_input" index="0">
                      <items count="9">
                        <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Which climate file to use. Click the widget and choose Weather file (catalog)… to pick from the catalog with a map and a spec sheet; the value stored is the file's own name (e.g. DEU_BE_Berlin-Tempelhof.AP.103840_TMYx.2009-2023). A station name, WMO id, country or dataset year still works as a search — the best match nearest the site wins — and a direct EPW or ZIP URL is downloaded as-is. A full address or place (e.g. the Site Picker's Address) that no file is named after is located on the map and the nearest file taken. Choose Open-Meteo (ERA5) — or type Open-Meteo 2019 — to build a file for the SITE (wire Latitude/Longitude, or set the Earth Anchor Point) from ERA5 reanalysis: one actual year, last year unless one is given, on the 0.25° cell. The same menu builds PVGIS (a typical year), NASA POWER (an actual year, from 2001) and ERA5 via Copernicus CDS (an actual year with measured radiation; it needs your own free CDS token, in the ECMWF_DATASTORES_KEY environment variable or ~/.cdsapirc, and the dataset licence accepted on the CDS website) and NSRDB (GOES TMY, a typical year, or GOES aggregated, an actual year from 1998, both PSM v4 on a 4 km cell over the western hemisphere; a release of Eddy3D ships a shared NLR API key for it, which your own (a free key from developer.nlr.gov, with your name and email) replaces: NSRDB_API_KEY, NSRDB_FULL_NAME and NSRDB_EMAIL, or ~/.nsrdbrc). Credits: weather data by Open-Meteo.com (CC BY 4.0); PVGIS, European Commission Joint Research Centre; NASA Langley POWER Project; Copernicus Climate Change Service (ERA5); National Solar Radiation Database (NLR). Terms: THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.</item>
                        <item name="InstanceGuid" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">47615860-adc4-43df-a275-183fffe7b21e</item>
                        <item name="Multiple" type_name="gh_bool" type_code="1">false</item>
                        <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Station</item>
                        <item name="NickName" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Station</item>
                        <item name="Optional" type_name="gh_bool" type_code="1">true</item>
                        <item name="Options" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">OneBuilding (nearest TMYx)
EnergyPlus (nearest)
Open-Meteo (ERA5)
Open-Meteo (IFS 9 km)
Open-Meteo (CERRA 5 km)
Open-Meteo (ICON-D2 2 km)
Open-Meteo (HRRR 3 km)
Open-Meteo (ERA5 + satellite radiation)
PVGIS (typical year)
NASA POWER (MERRA-2 + CERES)
ERA5 via Copernicus CDS
NSRDB (GOES TMY, PSM v4)
NSRDB (GOES aggregated, PSM v4)</item>
                        <item name="SourceCount" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">0</item>
                        <item name="Value" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">OneBuilding (nearest TMYx)</item>
                      </items>
